From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giulio Milani <giulio.milani99@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:29:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Mac: read wired Xbox One pads over USB too

A Xbox One pad that is not one of Microsoft's, such as the Turtle Beach REACT-R (10f5:7024), has no driver on macOS either: GameController does not list it and it has no HID interface. It speaks the Gaming Input Protocol (GIP) over a vendor-specific ff/47/d0 device, and sends nothing until it is told to power on.

The reader that already reads a wired Xbox 360 pad (pad_xusb_mac.h) now watches for both. For an Xbox One pad it claims the ff/47/d0 interface that has both an interrupt IN and an interrupt OUT endpoint (its audio interfaces have none), sends the usual power-on command 05 20 00 01 00 once, and reads the input reports (command 0x20: the buttons, two 10-bit triggers and four int16 sticks). pad_gip_parse turns a report into the same form as an Xbox 360 report, so the buttons by position, the dead zone and the trigger threshold are the same for both, and tests/t_pad_xusb.c checks it with reports captured from the pad.

Checked on a Turtle Beach REACT-R next to a PDP Xbox 360 pad on macOS 27.2: both pads at once, every button, the D-pad, both triggers and both sticks to full deflection, and one pad unplugged while the other kept playing. Not read: a pad that needs the Xbox One authentication handshake, rumble, the player light, the Guide button.

Upstream-Status: forwarded seedds/nxnative#2436
Reason: Xbox One pads that macOS has no driver for, such as a Turtle Beach, could not be a player
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#1197
